-- Doc 01 §8's control flow, as doc 18 §1.1 leaves it — plan 20.
--
-- Doc 18 deletes `cond`, `unless` and every postfix form, so what is left is
-- `if`, `while`, `until`, `for`, `loop`, `do`, `break` and `continue`. Each
-- one lowers to the Rust construct of the same name; `until` is the only
-- rewrite.
--
-- Plus assignment, at the bottom: `:=` and the five compound operators, which
-- belong here because a loop that cannot rebind is the limit plan 20 §10.2
-- disclosed about *this* file.
--
-- Checked by compiling and **running** with zero rustc output, because the
-- one thing a golden cannot catch here is an `until` parenthesised wrongly.

-- `if` with an indented block is a statement, and `else if` chains as Rust's
-- does — an `else` whose branch is another `if`.
classify : i32 -> ()
classify n =
  if n > 0
    println! \-> "positive"
  else if n < 0
    println! \-> "negative"
  else
    println! \-> "zero"
  ()

-- The same `if` as a function's whole body, which is where it carries a
-- value rather than an effect.
--
-- This was `tier n = if n > 9 then "big" else "small"` until plan 55 leaf 7
-- retired `then`. There is **one** `if` now: leaf 1 merged the two nodes and
-- left an `is_inline` bit to record which of the two spellings the author
-- wrote, and leaf 7 removed the second spelling, so the bit had nothing left
-- to say. Narrow branches still *lower* to one line of Rust — that is
-- `one_line_if`, and it is rustfmt's decision rather than the author's.
tier : i32 -> &'static str
tier n =
  if n > 9
    "big"
  else
    "small"

-- And an `if` used as a value — plan 55 leaf 2. Until it, this was `RDX0101`:
-- `prefix` reached only the one-line `then` production, so an indented `if`
-- was available as a function's tail and nowhere else.
--
-- The `else if` rung is here because it is the case that proves the chain
-- survives the change. Each branch is a tail position, which is leaf 0's
-- `tail` parameter arriving through `control_text` — and `control_text` passed
-- `false` until this leaf made the position reachable, so these three branches
-- emitted `"big";` and earned `E0308`.
labelled : i32 -> &'static str
labelled n =
  out = if n > 9
    "big"
  else if n > 4
    "middling"
  else
    "small"
  out

-- A conditional reaching a string interpolation, which is the one position
-- where plan 55 leaf 7 cost an author a line.
--
-- This read `"#{if n > 9 then "big" else "small"}"`, and plan 55 §10.8 priced
-- the retirement of `then` against *parenthesised* argument position, which
-- plan 65 then made writable (see `bracketed_block` below). It does **not**
-- reach here: `#{…}` is scanned inside a single string token, so there is no
-- line for an indented branch to go on at any bracket depth. A conditional
-- in an interpolation is now written as a call to one, which is what this is.
bracketed : i32 -> String
bracketed n = "#{tier n}"

-- And the same conditional as a parenthesised argument, indented — plan 65.
-- A `(`, `[` or `{` now opens a layout *context* rather than suppressing
-- layout, so the lines inside one carry `Newline`, `Indent` and `Dedent` and
-- `statement_block` has the `Indent` it needs. The closer pops every level the
-- interior opened, which is what keeps a continuation aligned under its opener
-- — `m = f (a,` with `b` under the paren — from matching no enclosing level and
-- earning `RDX0002`.
--
-- This is the function plan 55 leaf 7 needs: with it, retiring `then` costs an
-- author nothing in argument position.
-- `show` rather than a bare macro call, because plan 64 retired the one
-- built-in that took an arbitrary expression in argument position.
-- `println!`'s first argument is a Rust format string, so an `if` reaches a
-- macro through a name or through an interpolation — and an interpolation is
-- one line, which this deliberately is not. Argument position is still
-- argument position, which is what this function is a witness for.
show : &str -> ()
show s =
  println! \-> "#{s}"
  ()

bracketed_block : i32 -> ()
bracketed_block n =
  show (if n > 9
    "big"
  else
    "small")
  ()

-- `until c` is `while !(c)`, **parenthesised**. This is the case the run
-- catches and the text cannot: stop once `n` is over 5 *and* even.
--
--   `while !(n > 5 && n % 2 == 0)`  runs to n = 6   — correct
--   `while !(n > 5) && n % 2 == 0`  stops at n = 1  — the missing parentheses
--
-- The two agree on the first iteration and part company on the second, so
-- only the printed value tells them apart.
settle : i32
settle =
  mut n = 0
  until n > 5 && n % 2 == 0
    n += 1
  n

-- `for x in xs` is Rust's own, restored by doc 30 §3.2 — doc 18 §3 had taken
-- `in` away in favour of `<-`, and `<-` now means `await` and nothing else.
-- A range needs no special case: `1 .. 4` is
-- an ordinary expression, and the receiver rule parenthesises it where `.`
-- would otherwise bind tighter.
count_up : ()
count_up =
  for c in 1 .. 4
    println! \-> "#{c}"
  ()

-- A label goes before the keyword and reaches a `break` in a nested loop —
-- the one thing an unlabelled `break` cannot do, since it leaves the inner
-- loop only.
nested : i32
nested =
  mut seen = 0
  'outer loop
    for d in 1 .. 9
      seen += 1
      if d > 2
        break 'outer
  seen

-- An indented block is an expression, and `'name` labels one — which is how
-- a value leaves a loop that is not the last expression of a function.
--
-- Written `do 'search` until plan 55 leaf 7. §1.7's argument is that a
-- keyword announcing a block is redundant once a block is an expression; the
-- label is the one thing `do` carried that nothing else provides, so it stays
-- and is Rust's own `'search: { … }`. The word `do` remains **reserved** and
-- is `RDX0114` — `tests/ui/reserved_do.sdx` has it — because plan 60 is about
-- to give `<-` a monadic reading and the Haskell meaning of `do` is the one
-- still unclaimed.
first_square_over : i32 -> i32
first_square_over limit = 'search
  for c in 1 .. 20
    if c * c > limit
      break 'search c
  0

-- `return` inside a loop inside a function needs nothing from this plan: the
-- statement already existed, and a loop body is a statement list like any
-- other. Asserted rather than assumed.
first_divisor : i32 -> i32
first_divisor n =
  for d in 2 .. 20
    if n % d == 0
      return d
  n

-- `continue` skips the rest of an iteration; labelled, it skips to the next
-- turn of an outer loop.
odd_sum : i32
odd_sum =
  mut total = 0
  for c in 1 .. 10
    if c % 2 == 0
      continue
    total += c
  total

-- Assignment — plan 54's `:=`, and the two shapes plan 20 §10.2 disclosed
-- this file could not write: *"a loop can advance a numeric accumulator and
-- cannot rebind anything else."* Every mutating loop above uses `+=` because
-- that was the only assignment there was.
--
-- `=` still **binds**: at statement level `x = v` introduces a name, which is
-- why a second token was needed rather than an analysis. `:=` assigns, and is
-- unambiguous wherever it appears.
--
-- A swap is the case that cannot be faked with a compound operator at all,
-- and it is written as Rust's destructuring assignment — which falls out of
-- the assignment target being a general expression and which plan 54 §6
-- declined before measuring that it was free. `(1 + 2) := 3` is rustc's
-- `E0070`, so what is and is not a place stays rustc's judgement, per
-- AGENTS.md.
swapped : String
swapped =
  mut a = 1
  mut b = 2
  (a, b) := (b, a)
  "#{a}#{b}"

-- The non-numeric conditional update. `out += "big"` would append rather than
-- replace, and no compound operator replaces — which is the half of the
-- disclosed limit that mattered.
label : i32 -> &'static str
label n =
  mut out = "small"
  if n > 10
    out := "big"
  out

-- A field, an index and a deref target, because `*slot := v` and `row[i] := v`
-- are the shapes a loop actually needs and leaving them out would deliver the
-- row half-way in exactly the manner plan 20 §10.1 describes.
places : i32
places =
  mut v = .to_vec ([1, 2, 3])
  v[0] := 9
  mut n = 0
  r = &mut n
  *r := 4
  v[0] + n

-- All five compound operators. Four of them — everything but `+=` — appeared
-- in no example and no golden before plan 54: they shipped in plan 20 and
-- were asserted only in one emit unit test, which is precisely the
-- undiscoverable-construct shape plan 51's `construct_coverage` exists to
-- report. It reported this one the moment the row was graded.
--
-- The arithmetic is deliberately order-dependent, so the printed value
-- catches an operator lowered as the wrong one: 20, then +5, -3, ×4, ÷2 and
-- finally mod 7 gives 2, and any transposition of the five gives something
-- else.
compounded : i32
compounded =
  mut n = 20
  n += 5
  n -= 3
  n *= 4
  n /= 2
  n %= 7
  n

-- Shadowing, which is what `=` keeps meaning and what makes `:=` necessary
-- rather than merely tidy. The inner `n = 2` is a **new binding** in a nested
-- statement list, not an assignment to the outer one, so the outer `n` is
-- still `1` after the block — which is why plan 54's lint deliberately does
-- not fire here. An author who meant to assign writes `n := 2`.
--
-- Nothing in the corpus shadowed anything before this function.
-- `docs/GRAMMAR.md` graded shadowing `PASSTHROUGH` against
-- `examples/expressions.sdx`, which holds exactly one binding; the nearest
-- candidate elsewhere was two bindings of `a` in two *different* functions.
-- A construct graded reachable and witnessed by nothing is what plan 51's
-- `construct_coverage` exists to catch, and this is one it would have caught.
shadowed : i32
shadowed =
  n = 1
  inner =
    n = 2
    n * 10
  n + inner

-- `.then` on an `Ordering`, which is what retiring `then` recovers — plan 55
-- §1.3, and criterion 2. While the word was reserved this method had no
-- spelling at all: `(b.1.cmp (&a.1)).then (…)` was `RDX0103`, *"expected a
-- field name, a tuple index, or a method name after `.`"*, so a comparator
-- with a tiebreak had to be written as a tuple comparison and the new-program
-- corpus says so in a comment.
--
-- The input is ordered so that the tiebreak is the only thing that decides
-- the answer: `sort_by` is **stable**, so dropping `.then (…)` leaves `3`
-- before `1` and prints `31` where this prints `13`. A text golden cannot
-- catch that; the run can.
--
-- §1.3 and §3 case 1 both quote this as `(b.1.cmp (&a.1)).then (…)`, and the
-- **parentheses are not needed**: a method call is a receiver, so `.then`
-- binds to the whole of `cmp (&a.1)` and the two spellings emit the same
-- Rust. `redox fmt` is what says so — it removed them, and
-- `formatting_preserves_meaning` then held the claim to the AST.
ordered : String
ordered =
  mut rows = [(3, 2), (1, 2), (2, 9)] : Vec<(i32, i32)>
  rows
    |> .sort_by (\a b -> b.1 |> .cmp (&a.1) |> .then (a.0 |> .cmp (&b.0)))
  "#{rows[1].0}#{rows[2].0}"

main : ()
main =
  classify 3
  classify (0 - 3)
  classify 0
  println! \-> "#{(tier 4)}"
  println! \-> "#{(tier 40)}"
  println! \-> "#{(labelled 20)}"
  println! \-> "#{(labelled 7)}"
  println! \-> "#{(labelled 1)}"
  println! \-> "#{(bracketed 20)}"
  bracketed_block 20
  bracketed_block 1
  println! \-> "#{(settle ())}"
  count_up ()
  println! \-> "#{(nested ())}"
  println! \-> "#{(first_square_over 30)}"
  println! \-> "#{(first_divisor 91)}"
  println! \-> "#{(odd_sum ())}"
  println! \-> "#{(swapped ())}"
  println! \-> "#{(label 50)}"
  println! \-> "#{(label 1)}"
  println! \-> "#{(places ())}"
  println! \-> "#{(compounded ())}"
  println! \-> "#{(shadowed ())}"
  println! \-> "#{(ordered ())}"
  ()
